# E2E pool operations: always use run.sh

When managing E2E test pool VMs (con1-4, dis1-4), **always use `test/e2e/run.sh`** — never run `govc`, `virsh`, or manual SSH for operations that `run.sh` already handles.

## Command reference

| Intent | Correct | Wrong |
|--------|---------|-------|
| Power on pool VMs | `run.sh start -p 3,4` | `govc vm.power -on con3` |
| Power off / destroy | `run.sh destroy -p 3,4` | `govc vm.power -off con3` |
| Deploy code | `run.sh deploy -p 3,4 --dev` | `rsync`/`scp` to conN |
| Check status | `run.sh status` | SSH + manual checks |
| Stop suites | `run.sh stop -p 3` | `ssh conN kill …` |
| Run suites | `run.sh run -a -p 1-4 --dev --loop` | manual tmux/runner.sh |
| Verify pools healthy | `run.sh verify -p 1-4` | ad-hoc SSH probes |
| Reclone pool VMs | `run.sh run -a -p 3 -R` | manual govc clone |

## Why

`run.sh` handles VM discovery, power management, per-pool config, SSH user resolution, harness sync, lock management, and cleanup atomically. Bypassing it risks partial state, wrong SSH user, missed setup steps, or lock conflicts with the running dispatcher.

## When govc is acceptable

Only use `govc` directly for operations `run.sh` does NOT cover — e.g. inspecting vCenter inventory, checking VM info for debugging, or managing non-pool VMs (conno, disco, ESXi hosts).
